# Troubleshooting

## Overview

During the deployment of my Wazuh SIEM lab, I encountered several challenges related to package management, networking, agent connectivity, and service configuration. Rather than restarting the installation from scratch, I investigated each issue individually, identified its root cause, and applied the appropriate fix.

This document serves as a record of the problems I encountered, the troubleshooting process I followed, and the solutions that successfully resolved them.

---

# Issue 1: APT Lock During Installation

## Problem

While installing Wazuh, the installation stopped during the Filebeat installation phase with the following message:

```text
Another process is using APT.
Waiting for it to release the lock.
```

The installer kept retrying for several minutes before eventually failing.

<img width="652" height="267" alt="APT Lock Error" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/89050be7-b6f8-4bc0-990d-0603f7d32186" />

---

## Cause

Ubuntu's automatic update service (`unattended-upgrades`) was running in the background and had acquired the APT package manager lock.

Since the Wazuh installer also required access to APT for installing dependencies, the installation could not continue.

---

## Resolution

I first identified the processes currently using APT.

```bash
ps -ef | grep -E "apt|dpkg|unattended"
```

Then I stopped the automatic update services.

```bash
sudo systemctl stop unattended-upgrades
sudo systemctl stop apt-daily.service
sudo systemctl stop apt-daily-upgrade.service
```

I also terminated any remaining package management processes.

```bash
sudo pkill -f unattended-upgrade
sudo pkill -f apt.systemd.daily
```

Finally, I repaired the package database and refreshed the package index.

```bash
sudo dpkg --configure -a
sudo apt update
```

After rerunning the installer, the installation completed successfully.

