# Issue 5: NAT Networking Prevented Communication

## Problem

Initially, my Ubuntu virtual machine was configured with **NAT** networking.

Although internet connectivity worked correctly, the Windows host was unable to communicate directly with the Wazuh Manager.

---

## Cause

With NAT networking, the guest operating system is isolated from the host, preventing direct communication without additional configuration such as port forwarding.

---

## Resolution

I powered off the virtual machine and changed the VirtualBox network configuration from **NAT** to **Bridged Adapter**.

After restarting Ubuntu, I verified the new IP address.

```bash
hostname -I
```

The Windows Agent was then configured to communicate with the new Wazuh Manager IP.

---

# Issue 6: Windows Agent Became Disconnected

## Problem

After restarting the Ubuntu virtual machine, the Windows endpoint appeared as **Disconnected** in the Wazuh Dashboard.

---

## Cause

The Ubuntu virtual machine received a new IP address from the DHCP server.

The Windows Agent was still configured to communicate with the previous IP address.

---

## Resolution

First, I checked the current IP address of the Ubuntu server.

```bash
hostname -I
```

Then I updated the Windows Agent configuration file.

```
C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ossec.conf
```

Inside the following section:

```xml
<server>
    <address>NEW_MANAGER_IP</address>
</server>
```

I replaced the old manager IP with the newly assigned IP address.

Finally, I restarted the Windows Agent.

```cmd
NET STOP WazuhSvc
NET START WazuhSvc
```

After a few moments, the Windows endpoint reconnected successfully and appeared as **Active** in the Wazuh Dashboard.

